<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="cost"></A><B>How much does the game cost?</B><BR>
The original suggested retail price for the Windows and Linux versions
is the same -- $49.95. We sell the product from our website at
SRP to ensure that it will be available to anyone, anywhere.
Our site is not meant to be the primary source for our products.
We're working hard to make sure our products are available in
all the places where people buy software, and the retailers who
carry our games will have the ability to discount prices if they
choose.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
There is another issue involved in pricing over which we have
no control. Although the wholesale price for the Windows and
Linux versions is the same, some retailers may actually sell
the Windows product at a <I>loss</I>. They sometimes do this
with major hits in order to bring foot traffic into the stores
and thus increase sales of other, higher margin products. This
is something over which we have no control, and we simply urge
customers to shop around and find the best deal.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="intntlcost"></A><B>How much does the game cost outside
of the US?</B><BR>
We don't have any control over what retailers charge for our
games, but our suggested retail prices and wholesale prices are
identical in the US and Europe.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="buy"></A><B>How can I order or buy the game?</B>
<BR>
Please see our new <A HREF="/orders/resellers.php3">Distributor
and Reseller List</A> or <A HREF="/orders/">order the game online</A>.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="demo"></A><B>Will there be a demo version?</B><BR>
We do plan to offer one, but haven't yet set a timeline.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="upgrade"></A><B>Will I be able to buy an &quot;upgrade&quot;
to the Linux version if I buy the Windows version?</B><BR>
Loki and Activision sell their respective versions of Civilization:
Call to Power independently of the other. In addition, we pay
royalties to Activision based on sales of the Linux version.
We currently have no plans to provide a Linux 'upgrade' for purchasers
of the Windows version, but will announce an upgrade policy on
this website should that change.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="system"></A><B>What are the system requirements for
the game? How do the requirements compare to the Windows version?</B>
<BR>
The listed system requirements are the same. That said, we think
Linux users will enjoy all the benefits of Linux while running
the game. It's not emulated at all, but a fully native Linux
application running under X11.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<TABLE BORDER="0" CELLPADDING="0" CELLSPACING="0">
	<TR>
		<TD COLSPAN="3">
		<? echo $subhead ?>
		Minimum system requirements
		<? echo $_subhead ?>
		</TD>
	</TR>
	<TR>
		<TD VALIGN="TOP">
			<? echo $text_small ?>
			Linux kernel 2.0.x or 2.2.x<BR>
			GNU C Libraries (glibc) 2.x<BR>
			Pentium 133 MHz<BR>
			or PowerPC 133MHz<BR>
			4-speed CD-ROM drive<BR>
			32 MB RAM<BR>
			80 MB swap
			<? echo $_text_small ?>
			</TD>
			<TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</TD>
			<TD VALIGN="TOP">
			<? echo $text_small ?>
			400 MB free disk space<BR>
			X Window System<BR>
			16-bit color<BR>
			Network card (for net play)<BR>
			Internet connection (for Internet play)<BR>
			OSS compatible sound card (for sound)
			<? echo $_text_small ?>
		</TD>
	</TR>
</TABLE>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="3D"></A><B>Does the game require 3D acceleration?</B>
<BR>
No.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="console"></A><B>In what environment does the game
run? Does it run in X or from the console with SVGALIB?</B><BR>
The Linux version ships with support for X11R6. We may support
SVGALIB in the future and possibly GGI and MGL as well. If so,
a free upgrade will be available for download.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="Linux"></A><B>Can the game be played on all distributions
of Linux?</B><BR>
We have spent a significant amount of development time ensuring
that Civ:CTP works properly with all major distributions.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
The shipping version of Civ:CTP works with all glibc systems.
However, networking didn't function correctly on systems which
used glibc 2.1, but this has been fixed with the 1.1 patch, which is available on the <A HREF="/support/">support page</A>.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
Because the game is designed for glibc, libc5 users may need
to upgrade.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="FreeBSD"></A><B>Can I play Civ:CTP on FreeBSD with
Linux emulation?</B><BR>
This is not an officially supported platform. However, some have
made the game work on FreeBSD 3.1, by recompiling the kernel
to support Posix extensions.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="PPC"></A><B>Will you be offering support for PPC
and Alpha platforms?</B><BR>
The release CD contains i386 binaries and data files. 
We have posted to our website the beta binaries for PPC, which are available on the <A HREF="/support/">support page</A>. With the 
assistance of <A HREF="http://www.yellowdoglinux.com/" TARGET="_blank">Terra Soft Solutions</A>, we will release the final version of 
the PPC binaries. To play Civ:CTP on a Mac, you 
need to buy the Linux version of the game, and then download and install 
download the PPC binaries. You will need to use Yellow Dog Champion 
Server 1.1 or LinuxPPC 5.0.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
We are working on the Alpha binaries now, and will post them to our 
website when ready.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="ESound"></A><B>Does the game support Esound?</B><BR>
Version 1.1 has native support
for esound. Esddsp (a dynamically loadable object
which will redirect /dev/dsp to esound) did not work with 1.0
because it's statically linked. You can get the 1.1 patch on the <A HREF="/support/">support page</A>.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="XTerm"></A><B>Does the game fully support X-Windows?
Would I be able to run the Linux game using an X-term on a Windows
machine?</B><BR>
Some people have been able to do this, but it tends to be slow
and requires a very fast network connection between the two machines.
It is an unsupported configuration.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="HardDrive"></A><B>Does the game run from the CD?</B>
<BR>
It does not run from CD, and must be installed onto your hard
disk.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="install_maps"></A><B>How do I install custom maps?</B>
<BR>
The map is essentially a saved game. It should be placed in one of
the folders in ~/.civctp/save/games. For detailed information about
scenarios and maps, check out the Scenario page on <A HREF="http://www.activision.com/games/civilization/">Activision's
site</A>.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="tournaments"></A><B>Where can I get involved in Civilization: Call to Power tournaments?</B>
<BR>
Check out our newsgroup at <A HREF="news://news.lokigames.com/loki.games.civ-ctp.tournament">loki.games.civ-ctp.tournament</A> to meet other players and challenge them to a match.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="other_tips"></A><B>Where can I get more game tips?</B>
<BR>
Have a look at the <A HREF="tips.php3">game tips</A> page for more information about getting the most out of Civilization: Call to Power for Linux.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="WinSave"></A><B>Are the Windows and Linux versions save-game compatible? Are my Windows saved games playable on the Linux version, and vice versa?</B><BR>
The Intel version of Linux Civ:CTP 1.1 is save-game compatible
with the Intel version of Civ:CTP. Other architectures will not be
save-game compatible. This goes for scenarios as well.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="WinNet"></A><B>Can I play multiplayer under Linux
against users playing Civ:CTP for Windows?</B><BR>
The original Linux and Windows versions of Civilization (version
1.0) are not network compatible. All versions beginning with the first
patch (1.1 and greater) are network compatible, provided all network players
are using the same version of the game. For example, Linux Civ:CTP
1.1 is network compatible with Windows Civ:CTP 1.1.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<? echo $back2top ?>

<? echo $text_normal ?>
<A NAME="Binaries"></A><B>Would it be technically possible
to use the Windows Civ:CTP CD and then download the Linux binaries?</B><BR>
No, the games use slightly different data file layout, and have
some data file differences.
<? echo $_text_normal ?>

<P><? echo $back2top ?></P>
